<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Preserving Our Heritage – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Preserving Our Heritage</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What Should We Preserve in the Future?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;" colspan="3">
                <b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Recognize the writer's point of view in various texts. Reading: Analyze authentic non-fiction texts.
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;" colspan="3">
                <b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can recognize a writer's point of view in texts on heritage preservation and explain their reasoning with textual evidence (e.g., "The writer argues that historical traditions are crucial for national identity because they strengthen community pride."). Can analyze articles on cultural heritage, identifying examples that highlight preservation efforts (e.g., "Restoring Casco Viejo attracts tourists and promotes local culture."). Can summarize main points and viewpoints in articles about heritage, preparing for classroom presentations (e.g., "The author argues that preserving Casco Viejo is essential for maintaining Panama's cultural legacy and historical identity." "Another viewpoint emphasizes the importance of protecting indigenous traditions, such as the craftsmanship of the Guna molas, to ensure that future generations value and celebrate their cultural roots.").
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 45%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a large image of the Guna Yala Molas and asks students to quickly brainstorm words related to it for 1 minute in pairs. Students then share their words (e.g., <i>weaving, traditional, culture, identity</i>). The teacher asks, "Why are these Molas an important part of Panama's cultural heritage?" to activate prior knowledge and introduce the theme of preservation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to quickly scan a text for keywords and the main idea. The teacher briefly shows a short paragraph about the Emberá community and highlights how certain words (e.g., <i>preserve, traditions, villages</i>) indicate the central theme. The teacher then models how to identify an author's stated opinion by looking for phrases like "I believe," "it is critical that," or "in my opinion." The teacher will also model the pronunciation of long vowel sound /eɪ/ in words like "trade" and "great," and the reduced vowel in "important."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I want to understand the main idea of a text quickly, what should I look for?" (Expected answer: Keywords, headings, first/last sentences).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'writer's point of view' mean in a text?" (Expected answer: The author's opinion, perspective, or argument).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Preserving Our Heritage".</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive an authentic non-fiction article (e.g., an excerpt from a tourism board blog or a local news article) titled "Panama's Rich Legacy: Preserving Casco Viejo and Indigenous Traditions." The article includes descriptions of Casco Viejo, the Panama Canal, and Indigenous communities like the Emberá or Guna. It uses the target vocabulary (e.g., <i>heritage, sites, tourists, culture, restoration, villages, landmark, legacy, identity, efforts, preserve, attract, live, became, continue, restore, historical, traditional, global, critical, cultural, significant, colonial, pre-hispanic</i>) and grammatical structures (relative clauses: "Casco Viejo, <b>which</b> is a UNESCO World Heritage Site," "The museum <b>where</b> the history of the Canal Zone is explained"; Past simple vs. Present perfect: "The Panama Canal <b>opened</b> in 1914," "The Panama Canal <b>has attracted</b> many tourists"; Future simple: "We <b>will continue</b> to preserve").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading comprehension for specific information and inferring the writer's point of view in authentic non-fiction texts about cultural heritage.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> Students read the article quickly and answer: "What are the main types of heritage discussed in the article?" and "What is the overall purpose of the text?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read specific sentences from the text and decide if they are True or False. Example: "The Panama Canal opened in 1914, becoming a pivotal part of Panama's global identity."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match paragraphs to specific topics (e.g., "Efforts to restore historical buildings," "The importance of indigenous traditions").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Accuracy focus on Reading.</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Recognizing writer's point of view, analyzing textual evidence, understanding relative clauses ("which," "where"), differentiating past simple and present perfect for historical events, and using core vocabulary related to heritage preservation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled practice – Vocabulary and Grammar Identification:</b> Students re-read the article and underline all instances of the target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>heritage, restoration, identity, preserve</i>) and grammatical structures (relative clauses, past simple, present perfect). They then complete a cloze exercise where they fill in blanks in summary sentences using words directly from the article. Example: "Casco Viejo, ______ is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, ______ many tourists from around the world." (Expected answers: which, attracts).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled practice – Point of View Analysis:</b> Students work individually to identify sentences in the article that explicitly or implicitly convey the writer's point of view on heritage preservation. They highlight these sentences and explain in one sentence what the writer believes. Example: "The writer argues that historical traditions are crucial for national identity because they strengthen community pride." They also identify examples of historical sites and describe their significance using phrases like "Why is preserving historic landmarks like Casco Viejo important?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair interaction – Textual Evidence Scavenger Hunt:</b> In pairs, students find textual evidence to support specific claims about heritage from a list provided by the teacher (e.g., "Find evidence that shows the economic benefit of heritage preservation," "Find evidence of the role of indigenous communities in preserving culture"). They identify sentences that use past simple for specific dates ("The Panama Canal opened in 1914") and present perfect for ongoing impacts ("The Panama Canal has attracted many tourists"). They practice the pronunciation of words with /eɪ/ and reduced vowels while discussing.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Preserving Our Heritage".</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Paired mini-presentation/summary based on further reading.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students receive a second short non-fiction text (or a different section of the main article) about a different aspect of Panamanian heritage (e.g., "The Legacy of Panama Viejo" or "The Importance of Traditional Panamanian Cuisine"). In pairs, they read the text and prepare a 2-minute oral summary for the class. Their summary must include:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The main points of the article.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The writer's point of view on the importance of this specific heritage.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least two pieces of textual evidence to support their claims.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of at least three target vocabulary words and one relative clause (which/where).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They should consider: "The author argues that preserving Casco Viejo is essential for maintaining Panama's cultural legacy and historical identity." or "Another viewpoint emphasizes the importance of protecting indigenous traditions, such as the craftsmanship of the Guna molas, to ensure that future generations value and celebrate their cultural roots." They also aim to discuss heritage respectfully by acknowledging cultural diversity.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This activity allows students to practice reading for specific information and point of view, then communicating their understanding orally, preparing for classroom presentations.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Formative assessment strategy.</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during pair mini-presentations and written feedback on exit tickets.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will present their 2-minute oral summary of the second article, identifying the writer's point of view and supporting it with textual evidence. After the presentation, they will complete an exit ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist for Oral Summary):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can summarize main points and viewpoints in articles about heritage (Learning Outcome).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes the writer's point of view and provides textual evidence (Specific Objective, Learning Outcome).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., <i>heritage, legacy, preserve</i>) accurately and intelligibly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses relative clauses (e.g., "which is," "where people") correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows interest in cultural diversity and discusses heritage respectfully.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students write their answers to the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new idea I learned about heritage preservation from today's readings is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It was easy/difficult to identify the writer's point of view because..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What reading strategies helped me understand the texts better today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has my understanding of 'preserving our heritage' changed after reading these articles?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What aspects of identifying the writer's point of view did students grasp well, and what still needs more practice?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Were the authentic non-fiction texts appropriate for the B1.2 level? Were there any vocabulary items or grammatical structures that caused significant difficulty?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I better scaffold the task of analyzing textual evidence for the next reading lesson, or prepare them for speaking about it in Lesson 3?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson's focus on identifying viewpoints and textual evidence will directly prepare students for Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), where they will engage in debates and discussions about "What Should We Preserve in the Future?" using the information and arguments encountered in today's reading texts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>